                  <provenance type="#rejected" source="823"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_86">Bury St Edmunds,
                     Suffolk. Benedictine abbey of St Edmund, King and Martyr</orgName><p>This
                     attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great
                     Britain (1964). The corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index
                     card for this rejected attribution drew attention to James, <title>EHR</title>,
                     p. 256 n., but noted <q>No trace of Bury in it</q>. The Bury attribution
                     was listed with a query in the Medieval Libraries of Great
                     Britain Supplement (1987), giving the reason (p. 6, n. 3): <q>Bury
                     pressmark <q>J.12</q> altered to <q>J.13</q> top right of fol. 1r.</q> It is unlikely that
                     this is a Bury pressmark, since the known volumes of J.3
                     through J.20 are works of Jerome, and a manuscript from the abbey with
                     the pressmark J.13 is already known: Oxford, Bodleian Library, MS e Musaeo 112. See Richard Sharpe,
                     ‘<ref target="https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315096032-18">Reconstructing the Medieval
                     Library of Bury St Edmunds Abbey: The Lost Catalogue
                     of Henry of Kirkstead</ref>’, in Antonia Gransden, ed., <title>Bury St Edmunds:
                     Medieval Art, Architecture, Archaeology, and Economy</title>, British Archaeological
                     Association Conference Transactions, 20 (Leeds, 1998), 212.
                     The opening leaf of the volume is lacking,
                     but the pressmark is recorded on what is now the first leaf,
                     in a fifteenth- or sixteenth-century hand,
                     suggesting that the leaf was already missing by this time.</p><q
                     type="pressmark">J.13</q></provenance>
                  <provenance source="1003"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_105">Cambridge,
                     Cambridgeshire. Pembroke College (Aula Valence S. Marie)</orgName><p>The MS seen
                     by Thomas James at Pembroke College in 1600 (<title>CMA</title> 2103); his number
                     <q>175</q> in upper margin of fol. 1r. It was likely in the medieval
                     library, for Bale saw 'H. de Costesaye in Apoc.' at Pembroke (<title>Index</title>, p.
                     161). See Rodney M. Thomson, <title>A Descriptive Catalogue of the Medieval Manuscripts
                     of Pembroke College, Cambridge</title> (Cambridge, 2022), xx.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">William Laud (1573–1645), archbishop of
                     Canterbury.</provenance>
